#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ste-lint — ASD-STE100-inspired slop linter for technical prose. Stdlib only.

Checks markdown/plain-text prose against a Simplified-Technical-English-style
rule set: sentence length, a ~200-entry deny list (inflated verbs, hedges,
marketing adjectives, AI tells, nominalizations, phrasal verbs), passive voice,
punctuation symptoms, and synonym rotation. In SOURCE files it also flags
history in comments (dates, incidents, people): that goes in the dev diary. Skips code blocks, inline code,
YAML front-matter, URLs, link targets, HTML tags, and table delimiter rows.

Scoring: the primary metric is VIOLATIONS PER 100 VERBS (matches the published
research: baseline 4.36 -> STE 1.12). Verb counting has no POS tagger; the
heuristic is documented at count_verbs() and is honest about being ±15%.
A second metric, violations per 100 words, is stable and heuristic-free.

Exit codes: 0 ok · 1 --max-score gate exceeded · 2 usage/IO error.
"""

